WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.972
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll use

00:00:03.972 --> 00:00:07.125
encapsulation to protect an object's state and

00:00:07.125 --> 00:00:10.209
distinguish each object's fields from a field

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:11.580
shared by its class.

00:00:12.356 --> 00:00:15.737
Checked operations help inventory, booking, and

00:00:15.737 --> 00:00:19.046
account systems keep their records consistent,

00:00:19.046 --> 00:00:22.355
even when many parts of an application request

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:22.931
changes.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.989
Today we'll model a campus supply desk with a

00:00:26.989 --> 00:00:29.610
separate bin for each kind of item.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.979
A request must be positive and no larger than

00:00:32.979 --> 00:00:35.001
that bin's remaining stock.

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:37.921
A rejected request must leave the count

00:00:37.921 --> 00:00:38.670
unchanged.

00:00:39.411 --> 00:00:42.583
We'll put the checks inside the bin, create two

00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:45.823
bins, and report each request's result alongside

00:00:45.823 --> 00:00:48.659
the remaining stock and the number of bins

00:00:48.659 --> 00:00:49.469
constructed.

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:52.573
After a worked example, you'll predict a fresh

00:00:52.573 --> 00:00:52.911
case.

00:00:52.971 --> 00:00:55.871
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:00:55.871 --> 00:00:56.546
Main.java.

00:01:00.625 --> 00:01:03.603
A bin needs its own stock count, while the

00:01:03.603 --> 00:01:07.007
application needs one construction count for the

00:01:07.007 --> 00:01:07.858
whole class.

00:01:07.858 --> 00:01:10.979
Let's define SupplyBin and declare those two

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:11.475
fields.

00:01:20.392 --> 00:01:23.400
Private keeps callers from assigning these

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.546
fields directly.

00:01:24.546 --> 00:01:27.268
Stock belongs separately to every bin.

00:01:27.268 --> 00:01:30.491
Static gives the class one binsCreated field,

00:01:30.491 --> 00:01:33.141
initialized to zero when the class is

00:01:33.141 --> 00:01:34.000
initialized.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.008
It counts the constructions we record, not

00:01:37.008 --> 00:01:38.942
variable names or requests.

00:01:38.954 --> 00:01:42.257
We still need starting values for each new bin.

00:01:42.257 --> 00:01:45.419
The constructor will store its incoming stock

00:01:45.419 --> 00:01:47.879
and increase the shared count once.

00:02:02.029 --> 00:02:04.997
This dot stock selects the new object's field,

00:02:04.997 --> 00:02:07.513
while stock on the right is this call's

00:02:07.513 --> 00:02:08.158
parameter.

00:02:08.158 --> 00:02:11.125
The next assignment increases the class field.

00:02:11.125 --> 00:02:14.028
Our caller must supply a nonnegative starting

00:02:14.028 --> 00:02:14.416
count.

00:02:14.416 --> 00:02:17.512
That is a precondition: this constructor assumes

00:02:17.512 --> 00:02:19.254
it rather than checking it.

00:02:19.287 --> 00:02:22.488
Once a bin exists, callers need a safe way to

00:02:22.488 --> 00:02:23.270
take items.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:26.187
We'll give them a public take method that

00:02:26.187 --> 00:02:27.965
returns a Boolean result.

00:02:27.965 --> 00:02:30.953
First, let's reject requests that must not

00:02:30.953 --> 00:02:32.162
change the stock.

00:02:43.022 --> 00:02:45.985
The condition rejects either a nonpositive

00:02:45.985 --> 00:02:49.229
amount or an amount greater than the remaining

00:02:49.229 --> 00:02:49.653
stock.

00:02:49.653 --> 00:02:52.968
If either comparison is true, return false ends

00:02:52.968 --> 00:02:54.449
the call immediately.

00:02:54.449 --> 00:02:57.835
This guard protects our invariant, the rule that

00:02:57.835 --> 00:03:00.022
stored stock stays nonnegative.

00:03:00.044 --> 00:03:03.644
A request that passes both checks can now change

00:03:03.644 --> 00:03:04.994
the receiving bin.

00:03:04.994 --> 00:03:08.369
Let's subtract the amount and report success.

00:03:14.237 --> 00:03:16.774
The assignment reads the current stock,

00:03:16.774 --> 00:03:19.701
subtracts the accepted amount, and stores the

00:03:19.701 --> 00:03:20.352
remainder.

00:03:20.352 --> 00:03:22.889
Returning true reports that the request

00:03:22.889 --> 00:03:23.539
succeeded.

00:03:23.539 --> 00:03:26.336
The order matters: returning false after an

00:03:26.336 --> 00:03:28.873
earlier subtraction would not undo that

00:03:28.873 --> 00:03:29.654
subtraction.

00:03:29.654 --> 00:03:32.451
An exact request for the remaining stock is

00:03:32.451 --> 00:03:34.012
allowed and leaves zero.

00:03:34.032 --> 00:03:37.173
Callers also need to inspect the counts without

00:03:37.173 --> 00:03:39.111
assigning the private fields.

00:03:39.111 --> 00:03:41.985
Let's add a reader for one bin and a static

00:03:41.985 --> 00:03:44.257
reader for the construction count.

00:03:56.498 --> 00:03:59.625
GetStock returns the receiving bin's current

00:03:59.625 --> 00:04:00.052
count.

00:04:00.052 --> 00:04:03.321
GetBinsCreated returns the shared class count,

00:04:03.321 --> 00:04:06.022
so callers use the class name for that

00:04:06.022 --> 00:04:06.733
operation.

00:04:06.733 --> 00:04:09.860
Public readers make values available without

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:13.272
allowing an outside assignment to either private

00:04:13.272 --> 00:04:13.698
field.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:15.682
The bin definition is ready.

00:04:15.682 --> 00:04:18.957
We'll put the caller in Main, whose main method

00:04:18.957 --> 00:04:20.282
starts the program.

00:04:20.282 --> 00:04:23.627
For this worked case, create a three-pen bin and

00:04:23.627 --> 00:04:25.230
a separate one-map bin.

00:04:40.589 --> 00:04:43.889
These two new expressions run the constructor

00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:44.329
twice.

00:04:44.329 --> 00:04:47.702
The bins have separate stock values, while the

00:04:47.702 --> 00:04:49.462
class count becomes two.

00:04:49.462 --> 00:04:52.689
Requesting pens must not change the map bin.

00:04:52.739 --> 00:04:55.743
Let's request two pens twice and print the

00:04:55.743 --> 00:04:57.388
result of each request.

00:04:57.388 --> 00:05:00.177
The first request should leave one pen.

00:05:00.177 --> 00:05:03.467
The second asks for two when only one remains,

00:05:03.467 --> 00:05:06.614
so it must fail without another subtraction.

00:05:18.498 --> 00:05:21.676
Each print statement calls take before printing

00:05:21.676 --> 00:05:22.961
its Boolean result.

00:05:22.961 --> 00:05:25.801
We also need to inspect the stored counts,

00:05:25.801 --> 00:05:28.776
because a false result alone would not prove

00:05:28.776 --> 00:05:31.480
that rejection left the state unchanged.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:34.523
Let's add that report and finish the program.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:55.826
The complete report checks success, rejection,

00:05:55.826 --> 00:05:59.089
both bins, and the shared construction count.

00:05:59.089 --> 00:06:02.062
Let's click Run and see whether all those

00:06:02.062 --> 00:06:03.440
observations agree.

00:06:08.736 --> 00:06:11.915
The first request is true because two pens were

00:06:11.915 --> 00:06:12.592
available.

00:06:12.592 --> 00:06:15.704
The repeated request is false because only one

00:06:15.704 --> 00:06:16.312
remained.

00:06:16.312 --> 00:06:19.289
Pens is still one, confirming that rejection

00:06:19.289 --> 00:06:20.642
preserved the state.

00:06:20.642 --> 00:06:23.618
Maps is one because we never requested maps.

00:06:23.618 --> 00:06:26.798
Bins is two because we constructed two objects;

00:06:26.798 --> 00:06:29.436
taking items creates no additional bin.

00:06:30.198 --> 00:06:33.361
Now we'll use the same class with five pens and

00:06:33.361 --> 00:06:36.457
two maps, requesting three pens and then four.

00:06:36.457 --> 00:06:39.418
We'll enter a fresh program so you can trace

00:06:39.418 --> 00:06:42.177
both requests from those starting values.

00:06:42.177 --> 00:06:45.273
The guard and construction rule stay the same.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:32.674
The guarded operation is entered again.

00:07:32.674 --> 00:07:35.927
Let's finish its readers and create the two bins

00:07:35.927 --> 00:07:37.554
for the prediction case.

00:07:37.554 --> 00:07:40.469
Each run starts a new Java process, so this

00:07:40.469 --> 00:07:43.655
program begins with a fresh construction count.

00:08:06.517 --> 00:08:09.946
The caller now supplies five and two to separate

00:08:09.946 --> 00:08:10.875
constructors.

00:08:10.875 --> 00:08:13.876
Let's add the two requests and the report.

00:08:13.876 --> 00:08:17.162
Trace each request using the stock left by the

00:08:17.162 --> 00:08:20.449
previous one, rather than reusing the original

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:21.092
quantity.

00:08:47.893 --> 00:08:51.170
Before we run it, predict all five output lines.

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:54.379
For each request, decide whether the guard ends

00:08:54.379 --> 00:08:56.564
the call or permits subtraction.

00:08:56.564 --> 00:08:59.431
Then account for the untouched bin and the

00:08:59.431 --> 00:09:00.728
construction count.

00:09:00.728 --> 00:09:03.118
Pause here if you'd like more time.

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:14.504
Let's click Run and compare the report with your

00:09:14.504 --> 00:09:15.245
prediction.

00:09:20.963 --> 00:09:23.836
Taking three is true and leaves two pens.

00:09:23.836 --> 00:09:27.130
Taking four is false because four exceeds those

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:28.881
two, so Pens remains two.

00:09:28.881 --> 00:09:31.825
Maps also reports two, but for a different

00:09:31.825 --> 00:09:34.978
reason: no request changed that separate bin.

00:09:34.978 --> 00:09:38.271
Bins is two because the two new expressions ran

00:09:38.271 --> 00:09:39.813
the constructor twice.

00:09:40.631 --> 00:09:43.306
The supply desk now has one place to check

00:09:43.306 --> 00:09:45.280
requests before changing a bin.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:48.146
Private fields keep callers on that path, the

00:09:48.146 --> 00:09:51.140
guard preserves the stock rule, and the readers

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:52.669
let us check the result.

00:09:52.669 --> 00:09:55.662
Instance fields keep each bin's stock separate,

00:09:55.662 --> 00:09:58.719
while the static field records constructions for

00:09:58.719 --> 00:09:59.356
the class.

00:10:00.188 --> 00:10:02.976
Suppose the next request is for zero pens,

00:10:02.976 --> 00:10:06.095
followed by a request for exactly the remaining

00:10:06.095 --> 00:10:06.493
stock.

00:10:06.493 --> 00:10:09.612
What should each call return, what stock should

00:10:09.612 --> 00:10:12.599
remain after each one, and should either call

00:10:12.599 --> 00:10:13.395
change Bins?

00:10:13.395 --> 00:10:16.448
Explain which part of the method supports your

00:10:16.448 --> 00:10:16.913
answer.
